{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And now if, etc.</b> The verse's saying <b>and now</b> — according to the statement of the Sages of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 21) — וְעַתָּה means nothing other than repentance. For since Israel were sullied by the dregs of iniquities that had preceded them, and also the new ones that had recently come about through their rebellions — as the Torah records several details of sins regarding the manna, the quail, the Sabbath, the water — and when El Elyon wished to give the Torah, which is pure and clear, the foundation of holiness and purity and the spirituality of spirituality, He commanded them to return in repentance, even from a minor sin. And our Sages of blessed memory have already preceded us (Kiddushin 49b) regarding the law of one who betroths a woman on condition that he is a completely righteous man, and he turns out to be wicked — that his betrothal is valid, because we are concerned lest he did repent, and through his repentance he is deemed righteous. This will be further explained nearby." "<b>If you will surely hear, etc.</b> One must examine the reason for the doubling <b>shamo'a tishme'u</b>. Perhaps HaShem intends to command them regarding the acceptance of two Torot: one that He would cause them to hear at that moment, and one which is the Oral Torah and the precise rules of the scribes and the scrolls and the fences of the Sages and their enactments and their decrees that they would legislate, as alluded to in the commandment (Deuteronomy 17:11): ״לֹא תָסוּר מִכָּל הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר יַגִּידוּ לְךָ יָמִין וּשְׂמֹאל״. Therefore He said: corresponding to what He causes them to hear of the commandments then, He said <b>shamo'a</b>; and corresponding to the teachings that would come in the future from the mouths of Israel's Sages, He said <b>tishme'u</b> for the future. And according to this approach, what He said — <b>and now if shamo'a</b> — is precisely correct, for since He commands by saying <b>tishme'u</b> regarding the future, therefore He juxtaposed <b>and now if shamo'a</b> — to say: if you hear now, and you shall hear in the future, as mentioned." "And He was precise to say <b>in My voice</b>, according to the statement of the Sages of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 14): hearing from the mouth of a Sage is as though hearing from the mouth of the Divine Presence. That is to say: this commandment that I am giving you — that you accept the enactment of a Sage — must be equivalent in your eyes as though I Myself had caused you to hear it and declared it to you." "Or perhaps, through this, the statement of the Sages of blessed memory (Shabbat 88a) will be settled, for they expounded the verse (Exodus 19:17) ״וַיִּתְיַצְּבוּ בְּתַחְתִּית הָהָר״ — that He held the mountain over them like a cask, and said to them: If you accept it, etc., and if not, there shall be your burial. And it is difficult: did they not say (Exodus 24:7) ״כֹּל אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר יהוה נַעֲשֶׂה וְנִשְׁמָע״? What place is there for this coercion? And the Tosafot answered there that perhaps they would have recoiled when they saw the great fire that issued forth and consumed their souls — end of their words. But their words do not seem correct, for on the contrary, fear and trembling would increase and they would experience the awe of the Exalted. Rather, according to what I explained — that HaShem told them two Torot: one from His mouth then, and one for the future from the mouths of the Sages as mentioned — we can explain their answer, that they said ״כֹּל אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר יהוה נַעֲשֶׂה וְנִשְׁמָע״, that their intent was in this manner: כָּל אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר יהוה — the portion of it that is what He said, <b>and now if shamo'a</b>, which is the matter stated from the mouth of the Divine Presence then — נַעֲשֶׂה — we accept upon ourselves to do from now, before we hear it. And the portion of it that is what He said <b>tishme'u</b> for the future, which is the Torah of the Sage — וְנִשְׁמָע — meaning, they did not accept and did not refuse, but suspended the matter until they would hear, to know what Israel should do. For these are things that have no measure and no boundary for such acceptance, for in every generation they would introduce new laws and fences and safeguards and enactments, and who can sustain a Torah that has no such limit? Therefore they suspended it, that the matter would remain in their hands until they would hear and examine each thing, whether they could accept it. From then on they accepted upon themselves nothing but the Written Torah, but the Oral Torah remained pending. And over this HaShem held the mountain over them like a cask until they accepted upon themselves the Torah of the Sage — and it remained under coercion until the days of Mordecai, when they saw the life-giving handiwork of the Sage that Mordecai and Esther performed for them; then they returned to accept willingly to fulfill every matter that their Sages would introduce and enact upon them, after they saw the greatness of the deeds of the righteous, without whom no trace would have remained of the enemies of Israel. And although our Sages of blessed memory (Shabbat 88a) expounded their saying ״נַעֲשֶׂה וְנִשְׁמָע״ in a different manner, for praise — that they were like angels who anticipated saying the doing before the hearing, like the angels who anticipate (Psalms 103:20) ״עֹשֵׂי דְבָרוֹ״ before ״לִשְׁמֹעַ בְּקוֹל דְבָרוֹ״ — this is the way of derash. And even according to our approach, they did say ״נַעֲשֶׂה״ before they heard, and did not anticipate the hearing except regarding Torah of the Sage. Or it is possible that the verse intends both interpretations, one regarding the righteous and one regarding the rest of the people, for it is self-evident that in the ordinary course the ranks of Israel are not all equal in righteousness." "<b>Further</b>, the doubling <b>shamo'a tishme'u</b> alludes to what followed in truth regarding the acceptance of the Torah, according to the words of the Sages of blessed memory (Horayot 8a): that two utterances they heard from the mouth of the Divine Presence, and eight from the mouth of the angel that was created by His utterance, blessed be He. Therefore He said <b>if shamo'a tishme'u</b> — two levels of hearing. And He was precise to say, corresponding to the hearing that they would literally hear from the Divine Presence, <b>tishme'u be-koli</b> — and He did not say le-koli. Understand this." "<b>Further</b>, it intends to say that if they begin to hear the Torah, from then on they will themselves desire to hear more, and this is the verse's saying <b>if you will hear</b> — now I promise you that you will hear of your own accord once you taste its flavor, as it says (Psalms 34:9): ״טַעֲמוּ וּרְאוּ כִּי טוֹב ה׳״." "Further, it intends along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Sotah 21b), that Torah both protects and rescues, and this is the verse's saying <b>if you will hear</b> — meaning, if the hearing takes place, it will yield you two benefits: the first, that you hear His blessed voice, the words of Elohim which contain life, and you will live, as it says (Isaiah 55:3): ״שִׁמְעוּ וּתְחִי נַפְשְׁכֶם״; and the second, <b>and you shall keep My covenant</b> — meaning that it will protect you from the evil inclination. And along this line we have explicated the syntactic form of the utterance, whose proper place would be ״תִּשְׁמְעוּ״ and so on, that the essence of the mitzvah is hearing the Torah, and from it all benefits flow: first, that they benefit from the voice of the living Elohim; second, that they will keep the commandments and the evil inclination will not rule over them; third, ״וִהְיִיתֶם לִי סְגֻלָּה״ and so on. And according to what I explained above, that the verse's saying ״תִּשְׁמְעוּ״ refers to the Oral Torah, the syntactic form of <b>and you shall keep</b> and so on will be that through the engagement with Torah it will save him from the evil inclination that causes him to sin, and it said it with the addition of the vav, to say that besides your gaining knowledge from it to distinguish between good and evil, you will further attain the keeping of the commandments and the Satan will not rule over you." "<b>Further</b>, the verse intends along this path: <b>and now if you will hear</b> — meaning, prepare yourselves to hear, I will cause you to hear My voice — meaning that I will not speak with you through an emissary. It also alludes that through their hearing the words from Him, blessed be He, from a single hearing they will hear many further hearings, along the lines of the verse (Psalms 68:12): ״ה׳ יִתֶּן אֹמֶר הַמְבַשְּׂרוֹת צָבָא רָב״, and it will draw their soul with living waters, wisdom and understanding. And this is the measure of the verse <b>if you will hear</b> — meaning, a hearing through which you will hear many further hearings, is a hearing <b>in My voice</b>, and this is the verse's saying <b>you shall hear in My voice.</b> You have yet another benefit in this: <b>and you shall keep My covenant</b>, along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Shir HaShirim Rabbah 1:2 on the verse ישקני), that one who hears Torah from the mouth of a man is prone to forget it, whereas one who hears from the mouth of the Most High is kept forever and for all eternity, and this is the verse's saying ״וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם אֶת בְּרִיתִי״." "It also intends along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Shabbat 146a): Israel who stood at Mount Sinai — their impurity ceased. And the intention is that through HaShem's speaking to them in power, with the garments of kingship that He gave them, which is the human soul, and through this the soul becomes willing to abhor the impurities of the nations, which are evil desires, and this is the verse's saying <b>and you shall keep My covenant</b>:" "<b>And you shall be to Me</b> and so on. The intention here is that just as a סגולה (segullah) is something beyond nature — for you find that there is an herb whose nature is cold yet whose segullah is to heal cold-related illness, and likewise there is a hot herb and so on — and this is not according to nature but rather a matter of segullah; so too HaShem promises them that they will be a segullah even beyond what is reckoned." "And there are many branches to this promise. The first, after the manner of what they of blessed memory said (Shabbat 63a): if a person of Israel intends to perform a mitzvah and is coerced and does not perform it, they give him reward as though he performed it — but not so for a transgression; and among the nations it is not so, but the opposite. And this is not to be grasped according to reason, for whichever way you take it: if thought accomplishes a thing, then it should also accomplish for one who intends evil; and if it does not accomplish except in the aspect of good and not in the aspect of evil, then why among the nations should it be reversed? Further you will find (Sanhedrin 59a) that a Cuthean who engages in Torah or who keeps Shabbat is liable to death, and even the other mitzvot he is not to perform — and why, if the action in and of itself is a good action, should anyone who performs it not be doing a good thing? Therefore the verse says <b>and you shall be to Me a treasure from all the peoples</b> — for no good action issues except from your hands, for the special property of Torah and mitzvot belongs only to you. And the verse's saying <b>for all the earth is Mine</b> — meaning, one might say that this promise, that they would be a treasure, would not be justified unless the nations were desirous to learn and to perform etc.; on this basis it would be justified to say He made us a treasure. But I see (Sifrei, parashat Berakhah) that on the contrary, the nations refused to accept Torah and mitzvot when HaShem spoke to Paran and Seir — and if so, by what is the excellence of this exalted promise recognized? Therefore He said <b>for all the earth is Mine</b>: here HaShem made known that there is no nation in which there are not those who acknowledge His divinity, may He be blessed, as He says (Malachi 1:11): ״מֻקְטָר מֻגָּשׁ לִשְׁמִי״ etc. — behold, all the nations shall bow to Him, and even so they will not receive reward for this, and their actions make no impression. And this is the treasured quality of Israel." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that they be a treasure — and the treasure is that they be singled out for Him, in unification, to serve the Elohim of the universe more than all the peoples, for all the peoples too are Mine — meaning, their service is Mine, for they serve none but His ministers. How so? They serve the sun and the moon and the host of heaven — are they not all attendants of Him, may He be blessed? Thus they are found serving His servants, as the verse says (Deuteronomy 4:19): ״אֲשֶׁר חָלַק ה׳ וגו׳ לְכֹל הָעַמִּים״." "<b>And</b> what we find, that Pharaoh rebelled, and Sennacherib, etc. — that is by way of error and madness. To what may the matter be compared? To a king who hid himself and went out before them while they did not recognize him — he is like a commoner, and sometimes even his own servants will disparage him with the utmost contempt. So too Pharaoh did not know that He is this Master who exercises dominion over the one he considers his god. You thus conclude that all the earth bows to the ministers of His kingdom, and He chose for Himself Israel, that they serve Him in unification, and made them a treasure." "<b>And I will gaze</b> into the understanding of this matter that they of blessed memory said in Sifrei in parashat VeZot HaBerakhah, and this is their language: When HaShem revealed Himself to Israel to give the Torah, He went to the children of Esau and said to them: ״Will you accept the Torah?״ They said to Him: ״What is written in it?״ He said to them: ״You shall not murder.״ They said before Him: ״Master of the Universe, the very essence of their father was a murderer, and on this account his father promised him ׳וְעַל חַרְבְּךָ תִחְיֶה׳.״ He went to Ammon and Moab, etc. They said to Him: ״What is written in it?״ ״You shall not commit adultery״ etc. They said to Him: ״Their entire essence is immorality.״ He went and found the children of Ishmael; He said to them, etc.: ״You shall not steal״ etc. ״Master of the Universe, their father's entire essence was that of a bandit״ etc. — end of quotation. Now this statement is astonishing: what justification does a mortal have against El, and what argument is this — a deficient one — is it really an argument that holds up, just because their father was wicked? And further, one who ate garlic and his breath etc. [bears the consequences], and the difficulty grows: how did HaShem accept their argument, as is heard to contain substance?" "<b>However,</b> I will give you understanding: all that your eyes behold in our Torah, whether in positive commandments or in negative commandments, the reason for these things is that HaShem examined what He had created and recognized the aspect of evil and separated us from it, and examined the aspect of good and sanctified us in it. And this is the statement of the Zohar (Tikkunei Zohar, Tikkun 21) on the verse ״וַעֲשֵׂה לִי מַטְעַמִּים כַּאֲשֶׁר אָהַבְתִּי״ — from positive commandments; ״וְלֹא כַּאֲשֶׁר שָׂנֵאתִי״ — from negative commandments. And so it is the statement of our Rabbis of blessed memory in the Sifra (Mekhilta, on the verse ״כִּי אֲנִי ה׳ רֹפְאֶךָ״): HaShem said to Moses: Say to Israel — the words of Torah that I am giving you are healing for you, they are life for you — end of quotation. He doubled to say ״healing״ and ״life״ corresponding to negative commandments and positive commandments: healing — so that you do not become ill by eating or by doing the evil deeds; and life — which is in your performing the deeds that a person shall do and live by them. And you will find that when HaShem commands concerning forbidden things He says (Leviticus 11:43): ״אַל תְּשַׁקְּצוּ אֶת נַפְשׁוֹתֵיכֶם״, ״אַל תִּטַּמְּאוּ״ etc. (Leviticus 18:24), and He also mentions the forbidden relations by the name תּוֹעֵבָה and similar to these — to declare through the coming of the Elohim that all His thoughts are to guard us, to bring us into the aspect of holiness, and He has commanded us to be holy." "And you must know that every aspect of the act of evil has a root in the aspect of the spirit of impurity, and every aspect of the act of good is an aspect of the root of holiness and purity. Now, every commandment that HaShem commanded us concerning it not to do — its name is the name of the aspect of evil from which it comes. For example, the abomination of adultery — the root of the aspect of evil is thus its name; theft, likewise, thus is the name of the root of the aspect of evil. And at the time a person performs the deed of evil, behold, he sustains its evil root; and at the time he is careful not to do it, behold, he nullifies it — as they explained, of blessed memory (Midrash Tehillim 92), regarding the verse ״הִנֵּה אוֹיְבֶיךָ ה׳״ etc." "And know that a person's deed tells about the root of his soul — whether a root of wormwood and gall, which is the aspect of evil, or whether a root of the sweet and the pleasant, which is the aspect of holiness. And I have already written a branch of this in parashat Vayehi on the verse ״רְאוּבֵן בְּכוֹרִי״ etc. (Genesis 49:3). And in this we found illumination from the baraita we came upon, for an irrefutable argument was advanced by the three nations: Esav, Yishma'el, Ammon and Mo'av — each one according to what pertained to him. Esav said that the root of their souls is from that aspect of evil whose name is murder — from it comes his deed — and at the time of their accepting the matter, that is the negation of their existence. And likewise Ammon and Mo'av and Yishma'el. And this is what each one precisely formulated, saying ״כָּל עַצְמוֹ שֶׁל אֲבִיהֶם״ etc. — meaning, his entire existence is built from the aspect of evil whose name is murderer. And likewise they said of Mo'av etc.: עַצְמָהּ שֶׁל עֶרְוָה — meaning, that which you command them concerning it is it — meaning, they are its very aspect, so how could they accept the opposite? If so, that is their nullification. And likewise Yishma'el said ״כָּל עַצְמוֹ שֶׁל אֲבִיהֶם לִסְטִים הָיָה״ — meaning, the aspect of evil called listim (armed robbers), and their deeds would tell about the aspect of their soul, from what place it comes. Now, Ammon and Mo'av proved with their argument from the very root of their existence, which was from the aspect of adultery; but with Edom and Yishma'el the matter was not recognized from the root of their formation, rather Yishma'el through the angel who said (Genesis 16:12) ״וְהוּא יִהְיֶה פֶּרֶא אָדָם יָדוֹ בַכֹּל״ — behold, he tells her his root, for he is this; and Esav through Yitzchak, as he said (Genesis 27:40) ״וְעַל חַרְבְּךָ״ — meaning, you are from that aspect." "And I lift my eyes and I see the preciousness of holiness — He is the Master of Israel — who dealt with the nations in this order: to precede for each one of them a commandment against which they have a claim. For perhaps He knew that they would accept it since they have no claim against it, and the Holy One, blessed be He, acted wisely so that they would not take reward for accepting the first commandment against which they have no reply, and He opened His words to them with the exaltations of El, to dispossess them from the heritage of Shaddai, and chose for Himself Yah as a segulah for His people. Now, this is the meaning of His saying <b>וִהְיִיתֶם לִי סְגֻלָּה</b> — it is the decree of the verse. Accordingly, the reason He said it with the addition of the vav is for additional reward, besides the other particulars understood from the previous lofty attainments — you still additionally have that you will be a segulah as mentioned. And there was no need to mention them, for they are understood from the implication of the added vav of ״וִהְיִיתֶם״ etc. Further, the added vav may intend to hint at another matter: that their heart should not err to think that the Master, blessed be He, Heaven forbid, needs them for anything. It is not so; rather, a segulah is like kings who desire a segulah item, which is a precious vessel even though they have no need for it — so too you will be to Me a precious stone valued above all the nations, and not because the matter is a need of Mine, <b>כִּי לִי כָּל הָאָרֶץ</b> — meaning, from this point it is not fitting to say that I have need for the thing, as He said (Job 35:7) ״אִם צָדַקְתָּ מַה תִּתֶּן לוֹ״, for all belongs to Him, blessed be He." "<b>Further,</b> it hints at what they said, of blessed memory (Shabbat 146a): Israel who stood at Mount Sinai — their zohama (impurity) ceased. And this is their segulah-quality above all the nations, whose zohama did not cease." "Further, it hints at a supernal secret according to what has preceded us: that the branches of holiness were scattered throughout the world, and they have no way to be refined except through the instrumentality of Israel — and especially through the instrumentality of engagement with Torah, which is like a drawing-stone that draws forth its sparks wherever they are. And those sparks of holiness are also called segulah. And this is His saying <b>וִהְיִיתֶם</b> — we read it as ״וְהוּיִּתֶם״, meaning with aheh mela'um (a vowelized heh at the end) — for they will become the existence of segulah <b>מִכָּל הָעַמִּים</b> that were scattered there, through the instrumentality of Torah, as they said, of blessed memory, and as we have written several times regarding the matter of the plagues of Egypt. And His saying <b>כִּי לִי כָּל הָאָרֶץ</b> — here He hints that He has segulah scattered throughout the whole earth, and this is the reason for the scattering of Israel to the four winds of the world: to pursue the segulah that is their lost property. Now, but for the sin of Israel, they could have attained the matter without scattering throughout the world; rather, through the power of their Torah alone they would have ruled over the whole world and drawn forth all aspects of holiness from every place where they are. But through the instrumentality of sin their power wanes, and they need to go down there to refine that good."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {10 "The Sifrei citation 'רואיני' appears to reference Sifrei on Deuteronomy (parashat VeZot HaBerakhah); the content about Paran and Seir alludes to the midrash of the nations refusing the Torah.", 17 "The phrase להשמידם מנחלת שדי is translated literally; it appears to be a liturgical allusion blending language of dispossession with the divine name Shaddai.", 19 "The reading ״וְהוּיִּתֶם״ with בה״א מְלָאפוּ״ם (a heh read with a full/o-type vowel at the end) appears to be a phonetic or kabbalistic re-vocalization of וִהְיִיתֶם, translated here literally without resolving the mystical implication."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:31:43.754354613Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}